return array (
  'content' => '<p>Façade designers in India often choose between weathering Corten steel and conventional painted steel cladding. Both are steel; lifecycle, appearance and maintenance diverge sharply. Painted systems offer colour control and predictable factory finish; Corten offers evolving patina and reduced repainting — with run-off and variation trade-offs.</p><h2>Initial appearance and client expectation</h2><p>Painted steel arrives in exact RAL colour — uniform from day one. Corten arrives mill-finish, weathering toward rust tones over months. Client education prevents mismatch with marketing renders showing mature patina immediately.</p><h2>Maintenance cycles</h2><p>Painted cladding needs periodic inspection for chip, fade and recoat — coastal and industrial atmospheres shorten intervals. Well-detailed Corten avoids repaint but needs run-off management and occasional washing of adjacent materials.</p><p>Compare weathering behaviour in <a href="/blog/what-is-corten-steel-and-how-does-it-weather">how Corten weathers</a>.</p><h2>Cost structure</h2><p>Painted mild steel systems may win on upfront material cost for large industrial façades. Corten plate and careful detailing can cost more initially but saves repaint labour over decades on feature zones — full lifecycle costing is project-specific, not universal.</p><p>Avoid fake price comparisons; request quotations for your elevation area and fixing system.</p><h2>Design character</h2><p>Painted steel suits corporate blue, white minimalist commercial parks and colour-brand headquarters. Corten suits boutique hospitality, farm retreats and residential feature walls seeking warmth.</p><p>See <a href="/corten-steel">Corten applications</a> versus crisp PVD metal on entries via <a href="/studio/main-entrance-pvd-doors">main entrance doors</a> for mixed-material façades.</p><h2>Environmental and staining considerations</h2><p>Painted systems can chalk and flake — environmental disposal of recoating matters. Corten run-off stains stone if ignored — neither is consequence-free.</p><p>Drainage detail guide: <a href="/blog/corten-steel-facades-design-drainage-weathering">Corten façades and drainage</a>.</p><h2>When to combine both</h2><p>Many projects use painted steel on rear and service elevations while Corten marks public face — budget and narrative aligned.</p><p>Vyomika Atelier advises on Corten feature fabrication from our Delhi, India studio; painted systems may remain with separate cladding contractors.</p><h2>Thermal movement and expansion joints</h2><p>Painted steel panels and Corten sheets both expand under summer sun on Indian façades. Painted systems hide stress in concealed clips until paint crazes; Corten shows movement as subtle panel ripple if fixings are too rigid.</p><p>Allow slotted holes or proprietary floating clip systems on long horizontal runs. Structural engineer should confirm wind load and deflection limits regardless of finish type.</p><h2>Coastal and industrial atmospheres</h2><p>Salt-laden air near Mumbai, Goa and Chennai coasts accelerates paint chalking and edge corrosion on scratched painted steel. Corten can weather faster but may need thicker gauge and more frequent runoff control on light paving below.</p><p>Industrial zones with particulate fallout may leave visible dust on matte Corten — occasional water rinse helps without resetting patina. Painted steel shows cleaner after wash but needs touch-up where abrasions expose bare metal.</p><h2>Specification checklist for architects</h2><p>State exposure class (interior feature vs exterior rain-screen), desired initial appearance, runoff tolerance of adjacent materials and maintenance responsibility in O&M manual.</p><p>Cross-reference <a href="/blog/corten-steel-facades-design-drainage-weathering">façade drainage guidance</a> when Corten is selected. For painted systems, document recoat interval expectation without promising manufacturer warranties you have not verified.</p><h2>Handover and owner briefing</h2><p>Include patina timeline photos in client handover packs for Corten — not only mature European reference images. For painted steel, document RAL code and last factory coat date.</p><p>International enquiries are reviewed project by project — share location and elevation scope via our <a href="/contact">contact page</a> for fabrication feasibility.</p><h2>Substrate and fixing systems compared</h2><p>Painted steel cladding often ships as factory-formed panels with concealed clips on aluminium subframes — a mature supply chain in industrial and commercial sectors. Corten panels may use similar rain-screen logic but demand isolation from dissimilar metals and drainage paths that painted systems sometimes ignore when colour uniformity is the main concern.</p><p>Both systems need structural backup rated for wind and deflection. Corten adds the requirement that runoff and early weathering behaviour are communicated to landscape and paving trades before ground finishes are fixed.</p><h3>Fire and building interface</h3><p>Neither finish type removes the need for project fire strategy and compliant backup wall build-up. Metal cladding is one layer in a system — refer statutory compliance to the project consultant of record rather than inferring approval from material choice alone.</p><h2>Whole-life cost conversation with clients</h2><p>Painted steel may show lower initial supply cost on large flat elevations where patina variation is irrelevant. Corten often wins on feature zones where repainting would disrupt occupiers or where design intent depends on natural ageing. Present both as lifecycle choices with documented maintenance responsibility rather than implying one is always cheaper.</p><p>Request project-specific quotations for your elevation area, gauge and fixing method — avoid comparing brochure rates across incompatible systems.</p><h2>Project examples where each system fits</h2><p>Boutique hospitality entrances and villa feature walls often specify Corten for warmth at pedestrian eye level while painted steel covers larger service elevations out of sight. Corporate parks with strict brand colour requirements typically stay with painted systems because RAL matching and uniform recoat cycles matter more than patina character.</p><p>Mixed-material façades may combine Corten screens with <a href="/studio/main-entrance-pvd-doors">PVD entrance doors</a> — contrast between weathering steel and stable champagne metal can define arrival sequences without competing for the same maintenance logic.</p><p>When comparing systems on the same project, document the decision in the specification so subcontractors do not substitute painted panels for Corten feature zones without approval. Clear scope boundaries protect design intent through tender and site stages.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Choose painted steel when uniform colour and factory predictability dominate. Choose Corten when patina, texture and long-term repaint avoidance justify run-off planning and colour variation. Mixed façades often use each where it performs best visually and economically.</p><p>Explore <a href="/corten-steel">Corten steel</a>.</p><p>Read <a href="/blog/corten-steel-modern-facades">Corten for modern façades</a>.</p><p>Plan drainage via <a href="/blog/corten-steel-facades-design-drainage-weathering">façade weathering guide</a>.</p><p>For a project-specific quotation, share floor plans or photos through our <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Architects and interior designers may also begin from the <a href="/professionals">professionals collaboration page</a>.</p>',
